<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for "Bench Buddy.app".

  Read this honestly: with the ad-hoc signature this build uses (no Apple
  Developer account, see README), the hardened runtime is not enabled and most
  of these entitlements are inert. They are here so that if the project ever
  does acquire a Developer ID, signing with `codesign` under the hardened runtime option
  against this file produces a bundle that runs, rather than one that has to be
  debugged under notarisation.

  Why each is needed:

  network.client
      The whole point of the app: a raw TCP socket to the instrument on
      port 5025.

  files.user-selected.read-write
      "Save PNG" on a captured frame and CSV export from the data log both go
      through a save panel.

  cs.disable-library-validation
      A PyInstaller bundle dlopen()s Qt plugins and CPython extension modules
      signed with a different identity, or not signed at all. Under the
      hardened runtime, library validation rejects them and the app dies at
      startup on the Qt platform plugin. This is the standard, unavoidable
      exception for frozen Python applications.

  Deliberately NOT requested: camera, microphone, location, address book,
  calendars, photo library, Apple Events, JIT, or unsigned executable memory.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
